# Who included each file a unit reads, for the refusal of a file standing in
# for a header of the C library that only a group's header reaches (design.md
# §4.19, §1.12; panel 237's R2, defect 2343, 2026-11-08).
#
# **What it closes, measured on this Mac.** The refusal listed the runtime's
# opening alone (`cli/shadowed.hero`), so a library header that only a
# group's header reaches was read from a folder the build names unrefused: a
# program binding `stdio.h`'s `BUFSIZ` beside its own `_stdio.h`, which the
# SDK's `stdio.h` includes, printed 7 where the SDK says 1024; and a program's
# own `lib.h` including `<stdio.h>`, beside the program's own `stdio.h`,
# printed 7 and 14.
#
# **The refusal's one clang run lists the runtime's opening and then the
# program's groups inside the guard** (`text`), with `-H`, which prints each
# file clang enters with its depth in dots, `-fshow-skipped-includes`, which
# prints an include a guard or `#pragma once` skips as well, and `-MG`, so a
# group's missing header costs the listing nothing. The opening comes first,
# so a library header the runtime reads is entered beneath the runtime, and
# one only a group reaches beneath that group. **Every include of a file is
# judged, not only its first** (panel 243's R3, defect 2447), and a file is
# told once, by the first include that refuses it. A file of a folder the
# build names is then (`shadows`, the compiler-engineer's route):
#
# - **the program's own where the unit includes it for a group**: the header a
#   group names is the program's choice, read from where the program put it,
#   even one named like the library's (a `stdio.h` beside a group naming
#   `stdio.h`, the sitting's ruling); every other file the unit includes is
#   the runtime's, and one found in a named folder stands in for it;
# - **refused where a header that is no named folder's includes it**, the
#   platform's or the runtime's: `_stdio.h` beneath the SDK's `stdio.h`, and
#   the program's guarded `sys/types.h` beneath the SDK's `sys/socket.h`,
#   which built at exit 0 where a group of the program's had opened it first,
#   since `-H` alone lists a file at its first open and the rule judged that
#   open alone;
# - **refused where a platform folder holds a file of its name**, a header of
#   a named folder including it: `stdio.h` beneath the program's `lib.h`. Two
#   exceptions. A file a header names in quotes, found from that header's own
#   folder, is its own, beside it or not (`"timeb.h"`, `"../sys/timeb.h"`,
#   and `"sys/timeb.h"` from a header at the root), where C looks first, and
#   so is a sibling named as the search names it (`"sys/timeb.h"` from
#   `sys/clock.h`, found through `-I .`). A file of a folder only `pkg-config`
#   names is that library's, but
#   where its name is one of the C library's (`cli/shadowed_library.hero`,
#   panel 243's R2, defect 2428): Homebrew's libcurl ships `curl/curl.h`, a
#   name the SDK holds as well, and a program's own header including it was
#   refused. The angle form asks the system's places for a name the platform
#   holds, so a program's `money.h` writing `#include <util.h>` for its own
#   `util.h` is refused, its note naming the quoted form (panel 237's ruling;
#   CERT C's PRE04-C);
# - and a file `-M` lists and `-H` does not, found by `__has_include` alone, is
#   refused wherever a named folder found it, the landed rule, and told as a
#   file a header only asks after (`asked`, defect 2356): its words said the
#   C this compiler writes reads it, where nothing does.

use cli/deps
use cli/order_trial
use cli/package_reads
use cli/process
use cli/shadowed_library
use cli/shadowed_quoted
use cli/stand_in
use cli/strings
use emit/c_text
use emit/clang_place
use emit/macro_guard

# The words the refusal's run adds where it lists a group's headers.
#
# `-fshow-skipped-includes` (panel 243's R3, defect 2447): `-H` alone lists a
# file at its first open, so a guarded file of the program's that a header of
# its own opened first was never listed where a platform header included it
# after, skipped under its guard and read in that header's place: one order of
# two groups built and the other was refused. This Mac's Apple clang 21
# lists it, *"Show skipped includes in -H output"* (`clang --help`,
# 2026-11-10), and panel 243's critic read it in the reference of clang
# 18.1.0, the compiler's floor; a Linux clang and the Windows box's are unrun.
constant WORDS: [str]
    ["-H", "-MG", "-fshow-skipped-includes"]

# The runtime's opening, then every group's header inside the guard, as a
# unit reads them, the opening first.
function text(headers: [str]) -> str
    no_names: {str: bool} = {}
    lines: [str] @= [order_trial.opening([], question: "")]

    for line in macro_guard.around(c_text.includes(headers), bound: no_names)
        lines @ lines.push(line)

    return lines.join("\n") + "\n"

# One line of clang's `-H`: how deep the file was included, and its path as
# clang wrote it.
record Entry
    depth: i64
    path: str

# The `-H` lines of what clang said: one or more dots, a space, a path,
# written as a C string literal's text (`unwritten`).
function entries(said: str) -> [Entry]
    out: [Entry] @= []

    for row in deps.lines(said)
        line = clang_place.without_return(row)
        dots: i64 @= 0

        while dots < line.len() && line[dots] == '.'
            dots @ dots + 1

        if dots > 0 && dots + 1 < line.len() && line[dots] == ' '
            out @ out.push(Entry(depth: dots, path: unwritten(line.slice(from: dots + 1, to: line.len()))))

    return out

# **A path as `-H` writes it, read back**: clang writes each path as a C
# string literal's text (`Lexer::Stringify`), a `\` before a `\` or a `"`, so
# on Windows `build\\evil\\lib.h` is `build\evil\lib.h`, which the refusal
# named `build/evil//lib.h` and could not open to read its includes until
# then (defect 2359, measured on the Windows box with clang 23.1.1).
function unwritten(path: str) -> str
    pieces: [str] @= []
    start: i64 @= 0
    at: i64 @= 0

    while at < path.len()
        if path[at] == '\\' && at + 1 < path.len() && (path[at + 1] == '\\' || path[at + 1] == '"')
            pieces @ pieces.push(path.slice(from: start, to: at))
            start @ at + 1
            at @ at + 2
        else
            at @ at + 1

    return pieces.push(path.slice(from: start, to: path.len())).join("")

# What the rule reads of a unit's search: the headers the groups name;
# `named` and `others` as `stand_in.finder` reads them, `others[0]` the
# runtime's; and the folders only `pkg-config` names, each a library's
# (`shadowed_library.folders`).
record Search
    groups: [str]
    named: [str]
    others: [str]
    libraries: [str]

# **Each file of a named folder the tree reads in another header's place**,
# by the rule above.
function shadows(said: str, paths: [str], search: Search) -> [stand_in.Shadow]
    named = search.named
    others = search.others
    out: [stand_in.Shadow] @= []

    # Every file `-H` listed, which the `-M` pass below leaves to it, and every
    # file told, once: each include of a file is judged, since its first may
    # be its own and a later one a platform header's.
    seen: {str: bool} @= {}
    told_of: {str: bool} @= {}

    # The file each depth's last entry is, the unit's own at depth 0, so a
    # file's includer is the entry one level up.
    chain: [str] @= [""]

    for one in entries(said)
        while chain.len() > one.depth
            chain @ chain.slice(from: 0, to: chain.len() - 1)

        reader = chain[chain.len() - 1]
        chain @ chain.push(one.path)
        path = package_reads.lexical(one.path)
        folder = stand_in.finder(path: path, named: named, others: others)

        if folder.len() > 0 && told_of[path].is_err()
            seen[path] @ true
            name = stand_in.relative(path: path, dir: folder)
            told = refused(one, reader: reader, name: name, folder: folder, search: search)

            if !told.is_err()
                told_of[path] @ true
                out @ out.push(
                    stand_in.Shadow(path: one.path, folder: folder, named: name, reader: told.must().reader, quoted: told.must().quoted, written: told.must().written, asked: false)
                )

    for written in paths
        path = package_reads.lexical(written)
        folder = stand_in.finder(path: path, named: named, others: others)

        if folder.len() > 0 && seen[path].is_err() && process.path_exists(written)
            name = stand_in.relative(path: path, dir: folder)

            # A library's own file only asked after is the library's, as one
            # its headers include is (panel 243's R2).
            if !library_own(folder: folder, name: name, search: search)
                seen[path] @ true
                out @ out.push(
                    stand_in.Shadow(path: written, folder: folder, named: name, reader: "", quoted: "", written: "", asked: true)
                )

    return out

# What a refusal says of a file's includer: who it is, "" for the C this
# compiler writes; the spelling that would name the file in quotes from the
# includer's own folder, offered where the two lie in one named folder that is
# no library's, "" otherwise; and the includer's quoted spelling that found the
# file through the search rather than from that folder, "" where it wrote none
# (panel 243's R3: the message named the angle form for it).
record Reader
    reader: str
    quoted: str
    written: str

# Why a file `name` of the named folder `folder`, included by `reader`, is
# refused, or none where it is the program's own or a library's.
function refused(one: Entry, reader: str, name: str, folder: str, search: Search) -> Reader?
    if one.depth == 1
        if of_group(path: one.path, groups: search.groups)
            return fail(code: "own", msg: "the header a group names")
        return ok(Reader(reader: "", quoted: "", written: ""))
    from = package_reads.lexical(reader)
    home = stand_in.finder(path: from, named: search.named, others: search.others)

    if home.len() == 0
        return ok(Reader(reader: told_reader(from, others: search.others), quoted: "", written: ""))

    if !twin(search.others, name: name)
        return fail(code: "own", msg: "a name the platform does not hold")

    if library_own(folder: folder, name: name, search: search)
        return fail(code: "own", msg: "a library's own header, in a folder only pkg-config names")
    path = package_reads.lexical(one.path)
    at = shadowed_quoted.folder_of(from)
    spellings = shadowed_quoted.spellings_of(reader)
    searched = shadowed_library.held(spellings, item: name)

    # Panel 243's R3: a quoted spelling read from the includer's own folder
    # names the file, where C looks first, beside it or not; and a sibling
    # named as the search names it, the landed rule (shape `e`).
    if shadowed_quoted.found_from(spellings, folder: at, path: path) || searched && at == shadowed_quoted.folder_of(path)
        return fail(code: "own", msg: "a file its includer names in quotes, found from the includer's own folder")
    offered: str @= ""

    if !shadowed_library.held(search.libraries, item: folder) && home == folder
        offered @ shadowed_quoted.spelled_from(folder: at, path: path)
    written: str @= ""

    if searched
        written @ name
    return ok(Reader(reader: "`" + clang_place.slashed(reader) + "`", quoted: offered, written: written))

# **Whether a file `name` of the named folder `folder` is a library's own**
# (panel 243's R2, defect 2428): a folder only `pkg-config` names holds the
# headers the build asked that library for, its link taking the library from
# the same answer; a name of the C library's there is still refused, read in
# the C library's place.
function library_own(folder: str, name: str, search: Search) -> bool
    return shadowed_library.held(search.libraries, item: folder) && !shadowed_library.standard(name)

# **Whether a file the unit includes is a group's header**, by the path clang
# printed: the folder that found it, as written, then `/` and the header as
# the group wrote it, so `<./x.h>` and `<sub/x.h>` are their groups' as `<x.h>`
# is (defect 158's `./fixedbugs-158-angle.h` was refused as the runtime's when
# its name was read from its folder).
function of_group(path: str, groups: [str]) -> bool
    written = clang_place.slashed(path)

    for header in groups
        if written == header || strings.ends_with(text: written, suffix: "/" + header)
            return true

    return false

# An includer as a refusal names it: "" for the runtime's, which the landed
# sentence tells, a platform header by the name it has there, `<stdio.h>`,
# the C library's where C11 or POSIX.1 lists the name and the system's where
# neither does, the SDK's `sqlite3ext.h` among them (panel 243's R2).
function told_reader(from: str, others: [str]) -> str
    if package_reads.holds(dir: others[0], path: from)
        return ""

    for dir in others
        if package_reads.holds(dir: dir, path: from)
            name = stand_in.relative(path: from, dir: dir)

            if shadowed_library.standard(name)
                return "the C library's `<" + name + ">`"
            return "the system's `<" + name + ">`"

    return "`" + from + "`"

# Whether a platform folder, any of `others` but the runtime's, holds a file
# `name` names.
function twin(others: [str], name: str) -> bool
    at: i64 @= 1

    while at < others.len()
        if process.path_exists(others[at] + "/" + name)
            return true
        at @ at + 1

    return false

## Tests

# **A file of a named folder is told by who included it**: beneath a platform
# header, as the library's header that includes it; beneath a group's header
# of the program's, where the platform holds its name, as that header's, its
# note to offer the quoted form; never the header a group names, nor a
# sibling its includer names in quotes; and a file the unit includes for no
# group, from a named folder, as the runtime's.
test "a file of a named folder is told by who included it, and a group's own header and a quoted sibling are not"
    root = "build/selftest-shadowed-tree"
    assert !process.remove_tree(root).is_err()

    for dir in ["/rt", "/sdk", "/src"]
        assert !process.mkdir_all(root + dir).is_err()

    for pair in [["/sdk/stdio.h", ""], ["/sdk/time.h", ""], ["/sdk/string.h", ""], ["/src/_stdio.h", ""], ["/src/time.h", ""], ["/src/string.h", ""], ["/src/heroes_unit.h", ""], ["/src/money.h", "#include <time.h>\n"], ["/src/quoted.h", "#  include \"string.h\"\n"], ["/src/locale.h", ""]]
        assert !write_file(path: root + pair[0], text: pair[1]).is_err()

    said = [". " + root + "/src/heroes_unit.h", ". " + root + "/src/money.h", ".. " + root + "/src/time.h", ". " + root + "/sdk/stdio.h", ".. " + root + "/src/_stdio.h", ". " + root + "/src/quoted.h", ".. " + root + "/src/string.h"].join("\n") + "\n"
    got = shadows(
        said: said,
        paths: [root + "/src/time.h", root + "/src/locale.h", root + "/src/absent.h"],
        search: Search(groups: ["money.h", "stdio.h", "quoted.h"], named: [root + "/src"], others: [root + "/rt", root + "/sdk"], libraries: [])
    )
    assert got.len() == 4
    assert got[0].named == "heroes_unit.h" && got[0].reader == "" && !got[0].asked
    assert got[1].named == "time.h" && got[1].reader == "`" + root + "/src/money.h`" && got[1].quoted == "time.h" && !got[1].asked
    assert got[2].named == "_stdio.h" && got[2].reader == "the C library's `<stdio.h>`" && got[2].quoted == ""

    # A file `-M` lists and `-H` shows entered nowhere is one a header only
    # asks after (defect 2356); one `-MG` lists and no folder holds is none.
    assert got[3].named == "locale.h" && got[3].asked && got[3].reader == ""
    assert entries("x\n.. a/b.h\n.\n...c\n")[0].depth == 2 && entries("x\n.. a/b.h\n").len() == 1

    # Windows' paths come back as clang wrote them, each `\` once (defect 2359).
    windows = entries(". build\\\\evil\\\\lib.h\r\n.. C:\\\\Program Files\\\\Kits\\\\stdio.h\r\n.. a\\\"b.h\n")
    assert windows[0].path == "build\\evil\\lib.h" && windows[1].path == "C:\\Program Files\\Kits\\stdio.h" && windows[2].path == "a\"b.h"
    assert clang_place.slashed(windows[0].path) == "build/evil/lib.h"
    assert of_group(path: "inc/./x.h", groups: ["./x.h"]) && of_group(path: "inc/sub/x.h", groups: ["sub/x.h"]) && !of_group(path: "inc/ax.h", groups: ["x.h"])
    assert !process.remove_tree(root).is_err()

# **A nested header's sibling named in quotes from its own folder is its own**
# (defect 2420): `curl/curl.h`'s `#include "curlver.h"` read Homebrew's
# `curl/curlver.h` beside it and was refused as standing in for the SDK's,
# since the spelling was asked to be `curl/curlver.h`. Here `sys/clock.h`
# beside its own `sys/timeb.h`, a name the platform holds: `"timeb.h"` and
# `"./timeb.h"` are its own, and so is `"sys/timeb.h"`, which the search finds
# in the folder; `<sys/timeb.h>` stays refused (panel 237's ruling), and so
# does a quoted spelling that names another file than the one C read. And
# since defect 2447 (panel 243's R3) a file outside the includer's folder named
# by its path from there is its own (`"../sys/timeb.h"` from `lib/clock.h`),
# while `"sys/timeb.h"` from `lib/`, which C finds only through the search, is
# refused, told as written and offered the path from that folder.
test "a nested header's sibling named in quotes from its own folder is its own, and the angle form is refused (defect 2420)"
    root = "build/selftest-shadowed-nested"
    assert !process.remove_tree(root).is_err()

    for dir in ["/rt", "/sdk/sys", "/src/sys", "/src/lib"]
        assert !process.mkdir_all(root + dir).is_err()

    assert !write_file(path: root + "/sdk/sys/timeb.h", text: "").is_err()
    assert !write_file(path: root + "/src/sys/timeb.h", text: "#define TIMEB_OWN 21\n").is_err()
    named = [root + "/src"]
    others = [root + "/rt", root + "/sdk"]
    file = Entry(depth: 2, path: root + "/src/sys/timeb.h")
    verdicts = [
        ["sys/clock.h", "#include \"timeb.h\"\n", "own"]
        ["sys/dot.h", "#  include \"./timeb.h\"\n", "own"]
        ["sys/up.h", "#include \"../sys/timeb.h\"\n", "own"]
        ["sys/found.h", "#include \"sys/timeb.h\"\n", "own"]
        ["sys/angle.h", "#include <sys/timeb.h>\n", "timeb.h", ""]
        ["sys/next.h", "#include_next \"timeb.h\"\n", "timeb.h", ""]
        ["sys/other.h", "#include \"clock.h\"\n", "timeb.h", ""]
        ["lib/clock.h", "#include \"../sys/timeb.h\"\n", "own", ""]
        ["lib/found.h", "#include \"sys/timeb.h\"\n", "../sys/timeb.h", "sys/timeb.h"]
        ["lib/angle.h", "#include <sys/timeb.h>\n", "../sys/timeb.h", ""]
        ["root.h", "#include \"sys/timeb.h\"\n", "own", ""]
        ["root2.h", "#include <sys/timeb.h>\n", "sys/timeb.h", ""]
    ]

    for row in verdicts
        reader = root + "/src/" + row[0]
        assert !write_file(path: reader, text: row[1]).is_err()
        got = refused(
            file,
            reader: reader,
            name: "sys/timeb.h",
            folder: root + "/src",
            search: Search(groups: [row[0]], named: named, others: others, libraries: [])
        )

        if row[2] == "own"
            assert got.is_err()
        else
            assert !got.is_err() && got.must().quoted == row[2] && got.must().written == row[3]

    assert !process.remove_tree(root).is_err()

# **A file of a folder only `pkg-config` names is the library's, but where its
# name is one of the C library's** (panel 243's R2, defect 2428): the
# program's `own.h` reads a keg's `curl/curl.h`, a name the SDK holds too, and
# it is the library's; the same keg's `stdio.h` and `sys/socket.h` are refused
# (the ffi-pragmatist's `pcstdio` printed 7 and `pcsocket` 99 at exit 0 where
# every file was excused), and so is its `_stdio.h` beneath the SDK's
# `stdio.h` and its `sqlite3.h` beneath the SDK's `sqlite3ext.h`, the first
# arm, which knows no exception; a `curl/curl.h` in an `--include` folder
# stays refused, since only an answer of `pkg-config` pairs a header with its
# library.
test "a file of a folder only pkg-config names is the library's, but where its name is the C library's (defect 2428)"
    root = "build/selftest-shadowed-library"
    assert !process.remove_tree(root).is_err()

    for dir in ["/rt", "/sdk/curl", "/sdk/sys", "/src", "/lib", "/inc"]
        assert !process.mkdir_all(root + dir).is_err()

    for name in ["curl/curl.h", "stdio.h", "sys/socket.h"]
        assert !write_file(path: root + "/sdk/" + name, text: "").is_err()

    assert !write_file(path: root + "/src/own.h", text: "#include <curl/curl.h>\n#include <stdio.h>\n#include <sys/socket.h>\n").is_err()
    assert !write_file(path: root + "/src/two.h", text: "#include <curl/curl.h>\n").is_err()
    lines = [
        ". " + root + "/src/own.h"
        ".. " + root + "/lib/curl/curl.h"
        ".. " + root + "/lib/stdio.h"
        ".. " + root + "/lib/sys/socket.h"
        ". " + root + "/sdk/stdio.h"
        ".. " + root + "/lib/_stdio.h"
        ". " + root + "/sdk/sqlite3ext.h"
        ".. " + root + "/lib/sqlite3.h"
        ". " + root + "/src/two.h"
        ".. " + root + "/inc/curl/curl.h"
    ]
    assert !process.mkdir_all(root + "/lib/k").is_err()

    for name in ["/lib/k/opt.h", "/lib/stdbool.h"]
        assert !write_file(path: root + name, text: "").is_err()

    got = shadows(
        said: lines.join("\n") + "\n",
        paths: [root + "/lib/k/opt.h", root + "/lib/stdbool.h"],
        search: Search(groups: ["own.h", "stdio.h", "two.h"], named: [root + "/src", root + "/lib", root + "/inc"], others: [root + "/rt", root + "/sdk"], libraries: [root + "/lib"])
    )
    assert got.len() == 6
    assert got[0].named == "stdio.h" && got[0].folder == root + "/lib" && got[0].reader == "`" + root + "/src/own.h`"
    assert got[1].named == "sys/socket.h" && got[1].folder == root + "/lib"
    assert got[2].named == "_stdio.h" && got[2].reader == "the C library's `<stdio.h>`"

    # A platform header no standard lists is the system's, never the C
    # library's: the SDK's copy of SQLite's own header.
    assert got[3].named == "sqlite3.h" && got[3].reader == "the system's `<sqlite3ext.h>`"
    assert got[4].named == "curl/curl.h" && got[4].folder == root + "/inc"

    # Only asked after, with `__has_include`: the library's own `k/opt.h` is
    # its own, its `stdbool.h` refused as asked.
    assert got[5].named == "stdbool.h" && got[5].asked
    assert !process.remove_tree(root).is_err()

# **Every include of a file is judged, and the file told once** (panel 243's
# R3, defect 2447): the program's guarded `sys/types.h`, opened first by its
# own `sys/clock.h` as a quoted sibling, its own there, and then included by
# the SDK's `sys/socket.h`, the open `-fshow-skipped-includes` lists, is
# refused by the second open, told once and named by that reader, in either
# order of the two groups; a file the rule passed at every open is no file a
# header only asks after.
test "every include of a file is judged, so a platform header reading the program's file after its own header is refused in either order (defect 2447)"
    root = "build/selftest-shadowed-every-open"
    assert !process.remove_tree(root).is_err()

    for dir in ["/rt", "/sdk/sys", "/src/sys"]
        assert !process.mkdir_all(root + dir).is_err()

    for name in ["sys/types.h", "sys/socket.h"]
        assert !write_file(path: root + "/sdk/" + name, text: "").is_err()

    assert !write_file(path: root + "/src/sys/clock.h", text: "#include \"types.h\"\n").is_err()
    assert !write_file(path: root + "/src/sys/types.h", text: "#include_next <sys/types.h>\n").is_err()
    search = Search(
        groups: ["sys/clock.h", "sys/socket.h"],
        named: [root + "/src"],
        others: [root + "/rt", root + "/sdk"],
        libraries: []
    )
    clock = [". " + root + "/src/sys/clock.h", ".. " + root + "/src/sys/types.h", "... " + root + "/sdk/sys/types.h"]
    socket = [". " + root + "/sdk/sys/socket.h", ".. " + root + "/src/sys/types.h"]

    for listing in [strings.with_all(list: clock, more: socket), strings.with_all(list: socket, more: clock)]
        got = shadows(said: listing.join("\n") + "\n", paths: [root + "/src/sys/types.h"], search: search)
        assert got.len() == 1
        assert got[0].named == "sys/types.h" && got[0].reader == "the C library's `<sys/socket.h>`" && !got[0].asked

    # Its own at its only open, the same file is told nothing, and the `-M`
    # pass does not tell it as asked after.
    alone = shadows(said: clock.join("\n") + "\n", paths: [root + "/src/sys/types.h"], search: search)
    assert alone.len() == 0
    assert !process.remove_tree(root).is_err()
